import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  A list of two or more held in a FIELD typechecks but cannot be BUILT to evaluate, when what the
  spine joins over is a bare constant. @listFieldMultiple.ct@ holds the typechecking half and stops
  where this begins: it declares its values and does not print them, because printing is what
  builds them.
  What works, each checked on its own:
    - The same literals as an ARGUMENT rather than a field, printed and evaluated —
      @listHeterogeneous.ct@ and @listElemBound.ct@ both do this.
    - The same field printed with ONE element, and with none — @listField.ct@.
    - A field of two or more whose elements are DATA ARMS rather than bare constants, printed three
      lists deep — @listFieldNested.ct@, whose innermost array holds two @Json/Num@s with
      DIFFERENT float values, so it is not that the two cells agree.
  So it is the bare constant that is not carried, not the second cell and not the elements
  disagreeing.
  The failure is at tree build rather than in the constraints, and what it says is that the two
  sides spell the element in different currencies:
    Failed to build arrow tree
      from /Data/List/Cons(/head=/Data/String,
             /tail=/Data/List/Cons[/T=/Data/String](/head=/Data/String, /tail=/Data/List/Nil))
      to   /Data/List/Cons[/T=(CStr "x" + CStr "y")](/head=/Data/String,
             /tail=/Data/List/Cons[/T=CStr "y"](/head=CStr "y", /tail=/Data/List/Nil))
  The SOURCE carries the field's declared bound, [/Data/Primitive/String], and the TARGET carries
  the join over the literals themselves. Both are right about what they describe — the join over a
  spine's cells is how a list's element type is now read, and the declaration is what the field
  says it holds — but no arrow carries a value spelled one way to a slot spelled the other, so the
  build finds nothing. What is missing is the widening of a constant to its type on the way into a
  declaration-bounded field.
  The same shape with differing ARMS of a class fails identically, one level in: @Holder@ below
  joins to @Mixed/Tagged(/value=CStr "x")@ where the declaration gives
  @Mixed/Tagged(/value=/Data/String)@. Both are kept here because the two say together that it is
  the constant and not the arm.
data(Tagged(name -> String, tags -> /Data/List[$T -> String]))

every(Tagged.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

class(Mixed, [Mixed/Plain, Mixed/Tagged(value -> /Data/String)])

data(Holder(items -> /Data/List[$T -> Mixed]))

every(Holder.., isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShow])

# Printing is what builds these; declared and not printed, both typecheck.
> /Tagged(name= "two different", tags= ["x", "y"])
> /Holder(items= [/Mixed/Plain, /Mixed/Tagged(value= "x")])
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
